today we're going to talk about the do's  and don'ts of your first week in law  school and I know you're freaking out  that's okay don't worry about it because  none of this is going to be on the test  the first week of law school can seem  completely overwhelming just take a step  back and realize that it's all going to  be okay  everyone feels overwhelmed you're thrown  into a situation that you've never been  in before you're learning about subjects  oh you didn't know existed it's probably  going over your head you probably feel  stressed out you might be a little  freaked out that's okay everyone feels  that way but let's talk about all the  do's and don'ts of your first week of  law school everything you can do to  maximize your chances of success later  on and everything you can do to minimize  your stress in the present you should  understand that the way that law school  is taught is completely backwards you  learn the most antiquated arcane cases  early on in the semester and there is  very little chance that any of those  cases will be used on the final exam for  example if you are in property you might  learn about Pearson versus Post which is  the case where two men pursue two Fox  but one man killed it and carried it  away and it's a dispute about who had  possession of the Fox and the rule of  law is that mere pursuit of the animal  itself does not give you a right to the  ownership of the animal but guess what  there are almost no exams in the history  of property we're citing to Pearson vs.  post is a way to get points on the final  exam it's weird because strangely enough  pursuing animals is not something that  is often done in modern society and  therefore they both situations just  don't come up that much so don't worry  that every single class seems to start  with these antiquated cases that will  not be on your final exam and really  don't do much to further your  understanding of the substantive law so  don't feel bad if you feel completely  overwhelmed with the arcane law from the  court of Exchequer in England it's a way  to ease into the some  sure and it's not information that  you're going to be responsible for later  on the second thing to realize is that a  lot of the information that your school  is going to give you is bad for example  I know that it UVA  the professors give over an hour lecture  on case briefing how to highlight the  procedural posture of a case how to  separate out all the different parts of  a case that information is completely  useless case briefs are just a waste of  time and the information it comes from a  good place they want to give you some  idea of what you should be doing but it  really just Foster's busy work so don't  feel bad if the information that you're  given an orientation or the advice that  you got before law school is  contradictory or it seems like it's  creating more busy work than it seems  like the benefit that you're getting out  of it it's probably the case you're  going to get contradictory advice and  unless it's coming from us it's probably  bad advice so don't feel like you have  to do everything that you were told  before going into law school and don't  assume that just because your law school  tells you to do something you should be  doing it often it's the absolute  opposite of that case with respect to  case briefs I see professors giving the  advice that all students should case  brief every case that they get I think  that's really terrible advice in fact we  have an entire video on why case  briefing is bad and why you shouldn't do  it if you feel you must case brief do it  once and then put it aside I know the  final exam might seem like it's a long  way off but take a look at an issue  spotting exam and see if there is any  way that you can imagine that you can  use the information of a case brief in  the final exam odds are you can't and  I've been doing this for a while I've  never seen a case where using all of the  information in a case brief actually  helped on the final exam  I think it's just busywork it's a waste  of time next you're going to meet a lot  of people think about forming a study  group but don't put a lot of pressure on  yourself study groups can be great  they're not always necessary some people  learn better in groups some people will  feel like they waste a lot of time just  going on an endless conversation in  study groups feel it out perhaps start  up a study group but don't feel bad if  you don't want to join study groups  or if you feel like the study groups are  passing you by don't overcommit now is  the time to try things out I know it  feels like everything is coming down on  you and you're overwhelmed and slammed  but really you'll be able to separate  what's working for you and what's not in  the weeks to come in that vein  don't try to do everything know that  there's a limited amount of time that  you have and you'll want to really  maximize the things that give you high  return on investment things like  outlining things like practice exams  things like distilling and synthesizing  your notes with commercial supplements  but don't try and do absolutely  everything don't try to join every  extracurricular and don't join every  single intramural sports team there is  time for some of that stuff but don't  feel like you need to try everything out  at the end of your first week what I  would recommend is start outlining over  the weekend what I like to do during my  semester was use your weekends for  outlining distill all of your notes with  all of your supplementary material your  weekends are your time well during the  week you're going to class you're  preparing for class carve out your  weekends to start outlining and if you  outline every single week by the time  the study period rolls around and finals  you'll be studying from your outline  while everybody else is starting to put  together their outline now sure there's  value in putting your outline together  but there's far more value in getting  that out of the way so you can study  from it and start using practice tests  and remember even though it's week one  you want to think about the endgame it's  all about finals it's very rare the  class participation counts for anything  so remember to always focus on the  things that are going to provide value  at the final it's never too early to  make that realization and understand  that it's almost all about essays even  in classes that have some  multiple-choice questions the majority  of your points are going to come from  essays and in most first-year classes  almost all of the points come from issue  spotting long-form essays and now is a  good time to think about the strategies  that go towards better essay writing  because a lot of your points and a lot  of your final grade is going to be based  on an issue spotting exam because so  many professors use  choose spotting exams you can use the  same strategies in every class  regardless of the substantive law legal  offers an entire class on mastering  issue spotting exams and there are  others as well that are also very good  but it's never too soon to realize that  most of your grade is going to come from  issue spotting exams so the bottom line  is it's week one but don't freak out  you're going to have the entire rest of  the semester to get into your groove  everybody starts out in feeling like a  stranger in a foreign land very little  of what you're learning in your first  week is going to be on the test so you  can try different methodologies of  preparing for class and working on your  outline you can find what works for you  and then really get into your groove for  the rest of the semester  I know it can seem completely  overwhelming but that's okay it happens  to everyone  now is a great time to take a deep  breath realize that it's all going to be  okay and think about the habits that  will get you across the goal line at the  end of the semester if you like this  video please click like and subscribe  for more law school crushing tips and  also click below to download the free  checklist to help rush law school so  thanks thanks for stopping by  [Music]

